{0 Machines and states}

All behaviour in Silica is in machines. A machine is a statechart: a set of
states, the events that each state accepts, and the actions that each step
gives to the host. A machine is a plain value. It does not run by itself: a
host (the browser, the server or the checker) runs it. See {!Silica.Machine}.

{1:states States}

The states of a machine are the constructors of a plain OCaml variant. A
constructor carries the data of its state. A value of this type is a
{e snapshot}: one state and its data.

Each state has a name. The machine uses the name to know when it enters a new
state, and the checker, the spec and the ids of timers use it too. Give
[Machine.make] a function [~name] that gives the name of the state of a
snapshot. Match on every constructor, with no [_] case: when you add a state,
the compiler shows the function. Two snapshots with the same name are in the
same state, so a step between them changes only the data.

The example below is a field with a debounce. It has three states: [Empty],
[Typing], which carries the text, and [Settled], which carries the text that
stayed for half a second.

{@mlx file=examples/shared/doc_debounce.mlx[
open Silica

type event = Typed of string | Settle | Clear
type state = Empty | Typing of string | Settled of string

let name = function
  | Empty ->
      "empty"
  | Typing _ ->
      "typing"
  | Settled _ ->
      "settled"

let typed : string -> (state, event) Machine.next = function
  | "" ->
      Go Empty
  | text ->
      Reenter (Typing text)

let machine =
  Machine.make ~id:"debounce" ~init:Empty ~name
    ~during:(function
      | Typing _ -> [ Host.after 500 Settle ] | Empty | Settled _ -> []
      )
    (fun state event ->
      match (state, event) with
      | _, Typed text ->
          typed text
      | Typing text, Settle ->
          Go (Settled text)
      | _, Clear ->
          Go Empty
      | _ ->
          Ignore
    )

let view (state : state) : event Node.t =
  let text, note =
    match state with
    | Empty ->
        ("", "Type a word.")
    | Typing text ->
        (text, "Waiting for you to stop.")
    | Settled text ->
        (text, "You typed " ^ text ^ ".")
  in
  <div>
    <input aria_label="Word" value=text on_input=(fun text -> Typed text) />
    <button on_press=Clear disabled=(text = "")>"Clear"</button>
    <p>(Node.text note)</p>
  </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

{1:events Events}

The checker, the spec and the traces show each event with a label. A machine
does not need to name its events. With no [~event_name], the label of an event
is its cause (see {!Silica.Transition.cause_label}):

{t
| Cause | Label |
|-------|-------|
| A press or a key on an element | The handler, the key, the role and the accessible name: [press button "Save"], [key_down Escape textbox "Search"] |
| A timer | [after 2000ms] |
| The answer of a call | [ok /api/save], [rejected /api/save] or [failed /api/save]; [answer /api/save] when the trace does not record the answer |
| A subscription | [live <topic>] |
| A held resource | Its name, such as [dismiss] |
| The result of an action | [result <action>] |
| Other causes | [props], [child <instance>], [url <url>], [preload <url>], [message] |
}

To show your own names, give [Machine.make] a function [~event_name]. By
convention the name is the constructor in lower case. When the data of an event
matters to a reader of a spec or a trace, add a word for the data:

{@ocaml skip[
let event_name = function
  | Settled (Remote.Ok _) -> "settled ok"
  | Settled (Rejected _) -> "settled rejected"
  | Settled (Failed _) -> "settled error"
  | Select value -> "select " ^ value
  | Retry -> "retry"
]}

The [event] field of a checker step (see {!Check.type-step}) holds this name,
and {!Silica.Machine.event_name} gives it ([None] for a machine with no
[event_name]). The names are in every build, release too: production traces
record them.

A trace with names only (see {!Silica.Transition.names_only}) does not keep
the accessible names and the answers. So the conformance check
({!Silica.Spec.conforms}, [silica conform]) matches an event with no name by
its states only: the step must go between the same states as a step of the
spec.

{1:codecs Codecs}

The JSON codecs of the snapshots and the events (see {!Silica.Codec}) serve
traces, replay and development tools. They are not part of the machine: an app
gives them with {!Silica.Machine.register_codecs}, in one module that only the
development entry points call (tests, the checker suite, a development client).
A release client that does not call it carries no codec, because the bundler
drops the code that nothing references. In an app that derives JSON with
[[@@deriving json]], {!Silica_jsonkit.codec} makes the codecs:

{@ocaml skip[
type event = Save | Answer of (string, string) Silica_jsonkit.answer
[@@deriving json]

type state = Idle | Saving | Saved of string [@@deriving json]

let machine = Machine.make ~id:"save" ~init:Idle ~name update
]}

A module [Save_codecs], which only the tests and the development tools call:

{@ocaml skip[
let register () =
  Machine.register_codecs machine
    ~state:(Silica_jsonkit.codec ~to_json:state_to_json ~of_json:state_of_json)
    ~event:(Silica_jsonkit.codec ~to_json:event_to_json ~of_json:event_of_json)
    ()
]}

Derive the JSON on top-level types: the bundler drops an unused top-level
function, but not the functions inside a submodule that the app uses. The
example app has [Example_codecs.register]: its server, its check suite, its
tests and its development client call it, and its production client does not.

[Silica_jsonkit.answer], [Silica_jsonkit.update], [Silica_jsonkit.status] and
[Silica_jsonkit.error] are the types of {!Silica.Remote}, {!Silica.Topic} and
{!Silica.Status} under names that [[@@deriving json]] can use. Without the
JSON deriver, {!Silica.Remote.answer_codec}, {!Silica.Topic.update_codec} and
{!Silica.Status.codec} build the codecs of these types;
{!Silica.Remote_machine.state_codec} and {!Silica.Router.state_codec} give the
codecs of the ready-made machines. Leave out a codec when the events or the
states hold closures or other data that is not plain data.
{!Silica.Machine.event_codec} and {!Silica.Machine.state_codec} read the
registered codecs; for a machine that is not registered they give an [Error]
that names the machine and says how to register it. A test of the app can
check with {!Silica.Machine.registered_codecs} that the codec module lists
every machine whose events derive JSON, as the tests of the example app and
the site do.

Under [silica dev], the state codec also keeps the snapshot of a machine
across a hot reload of the page and a restart of the server: the old code
encodes the snapshot and the new code decodes it with its own codec. A
snapshot that does not decode, because the state type changed, starts again,
and [silica dev] says why; so does the snapshot of a machine with no state
codec, unless it is the snapshot that the machine starts in. Register the
codecs in the development client for it, as [example/client] and the client
of [silica new] do.

{1:make Make a machine}

[Machine.make ~id ~init ~name update] makes the machine. [id] names the
machine in the spec, in the checker and in the ids of resources.
[init] is the first snapshot. [name] gives the name of the state of a snapshot
(see {!section-states}). The other options:

{t
| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| [states] | The list of every name that [name] gives. The checker uses it to report the states that no run reached. |
| [event_name] | The names of the events (see {!section-events}). |
| [during] | The timers, calls and resources that run during a snapshot (see {!section-during}). |
| [entry], [exit] | The actions to run when the machine enters or leaves a state. They get the snapshot. |
| [final] | The snapshots where the machine stops: a final state handles no event. |
| [start] | Actions that run once, before the entry of [init]. |
}

A machine with no [states] cannot tell the checker which states exist. The
report of the checker then has a line [unlisted: ...] for the machine: it does
not report unreached states for it. Give [states] when a state that no run
reaches is a bug:

{@ocaml skip[
let states = [ "empty"; "typing"; "settled" ]

let machine =
  Machine.make ~id:"debounce" ~init:Empty ~name ~states ~during update
]}

The last argument is the update: a function of the snapshot and the event that
gives the next step. Most updates match on the pair and end with
[| _ -> Ignore]. A case that works in every state, as [Clear] in the example,
is a case with [_] for the state, before the last one.

{1:steps The next step}

The update gives a {!Silica.Machine.next}:

{t
| Constructor | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| [Go snapshot] | Go to the snapshot. When its name is the name of the current state, only the data changes: no exit, no entry, and the resources keep running. |
| [Go_with (snapshot, actions)] | Like [Go], and run the actions between the exit and the entry. |
| [Stay actions] | The event is handled: keep the snapshot and run the actions. |
| [Reenter snapshot] | Like [Go], but exit and enter also when the name does not change. All resources start again. |
| [Ignore] | The event is not handled. The snapshot does not change. |
}

A debounce is [Reenter] on a state with a timer in [during]: each new text
starts the timer again, so the timer fires only after the last key.

{!Silica.Machine.can} tells whether the machine handles an event in a
snapshot: the snapshot is not final and the update does not give [Ignore]. A
view can disable a button with it (see {!section-records}).

{1:during Resources}

[during] gives, for each snapshot, the list of the timers
({!Silica.Host.after}), Remote calls ({!Silica.Remote.call}) and other
resources that run during it. A resource starts when it comes into the list and
stops when it goes out of the list, so a timer or an answer of a left state
never arrives.

Each resource has a key ({!Silica.Host.key}): the path and the input of a
call, the topic and key of a subscription, the delay of a timer. After each
step, the machine compares the keys of the old and the new list. Each key
starts with the name of the state, so a step to a state with another name
stops every resource and starts the resources of the new state, even the ones
that look the same. In a name with a dot, such as [list.offline], the key
starts with the part before the first dot: a step between [list.live] and
[list.offline] keeps the resources (see {!page-data.phases}). It stops the resources whose key went away and starts the
new ones. The other resources keep running. So the calls of a state can change
with its data, and a call whose input changes starts again. See {!page-actions} for the resources.

{1:run Run a machine}

{!Silica.Machine.start} gives the first snapshot and the actions of the start.
{!Silica.Machine.step} gives the next snapshot, the actions and how the step
changed the snapshot: [Unhandled], [Kept] (same name, or another sub-state
of the same state) or [Entered].
{!Silica.Machine.active} lists the resources that run during the
snapshot.
These functions are for a machine from [Machine.make], which has no props.
For a machine with props, give the props to
{!Silica.Machine.start_with_props}, {!Silica.Machine.step_with_props} and
{!Silica.Machine.active_with_props}.

{@ocaml[
# let machine = Doc_debounce.machine;;
val machine : (unit, Doc_debounce.state, Doc_debounce.event) Silica.Machine.t =
  <abstr>
# let state, _ = Silica.Machine.start machine;;
val state : Doc_debounce.state = Doc_debounce.Empty
# let state, actions, _ =
    Silica.Machine.step machine state (Doc_debounce.Typed "oc");;
val state : Doc_debounce.state = Doc_debounce.Typing "oc"
val actions : Doc_debounce.event Silica.Action.host list =
  [Silica.Action.Start_timer
    {Silica.Action.id = "debounce/typing/after 500ms"; ms = 500;
     event = Doc_debounce.Settle}]
# Silica.Machine.active machine state;;
- : Silica.Machine.resource list =
[Silica.Machine.Timer
  {Silica.Machine.id = "debounce/typing/after 500ms"; ms = 500}]
]}

A second text enters [Typing] again. The actions stop the old timer and start
a new one:

{@ocaml[
# let state, actions, _ =
    Silica.Machine.step machine state (Doc_debounce.Typed "ocaml");;
val state : Doc_debounce.state = Doc_debounce.Typing "ocaml"
val actions : Doc_debounce.event Silica.Action.host list =
  [Silica.Action.Stop "debounce/typing/after 500ms";
   Silica.Action.Start_timer
    {Silica.Action.id = "debounce/typing/after 500ms"; ms = 500;
     event = Doc_debounce.Settle}]
# let state, _, _ = Silica.Machine.step machine state Doc_debounce.Settle;;
val state : Doc_debounce.state = Doc_debounce.Settled "ocaml"
# Silica.Machine.name machine state;;
- : string = "settled"
]}

{1:props Props}

A component that gets props from its parent uses
{!Silica.Machine.make_with_props}. The update, [during], [entry] and [exit] get
the props of the moment as their first argument. The props are not an event and
are not stored in the snapshot: the view gets them too (see
{!page-components.children}).

[on_props previous props snapshot] reacts to new props. It runs at the start,
with [previous = None], and each time the parent gives other props. It gives a
[next], as the update does, and defaults to [Ignore]. After it, the resources are
compared by key, so a call whose input comes from the props starts again when
the props change. A switch that follows a [checked] prop:

{@ocaml skip[
let machine =
  Machine.make_with_props ~id:"switch" ~init:Unchecked ~name
    ~on_props:(fun previous props _ ->
      match previous with
      | Some (previous : props) when previous.checked = props.checked ->
          Ignore
      | Some _ | None ->
          Go (of_props props)
    )
    (fun (props : props) state Toggle ->
      if props.disabled then
        Ignore
      else
        match state with Checked -> Go Unchecked | Unchecked -> Go Checked
    )
]}

{1:records States in a record}

Some data belongs to every state, such as the text of a form. Then the
snapshot is a record, and one field holds the variant of the phase. The [name]
of the snapshot is the name of its phase. A helper decides the step of the phase
and gives a [next] of the phase; {!Silica.Machine.map_next} puts the phase back
into the record:

{@mlx file=examples/shared/doc_signup.mlx[
open Silica

let subscribe =
  Remote.declare ~path:"/api/subscribe" ~input:Codec.string ~output:Codec.string
    ~rejected:Codec.string

type event = Typed of string | Send | Answer of (string, string) Remote.answer
type phase = Editing | Sending | Sent of string | Failed of string

type state = { email : string; phase : phase }

let phase_step email phase event : (phase, event) Machine.next =
  match (phase, event) with
  | (Editing | Failed _), Send when String.contains email '@' ->
      Go Sending
  | Sending, Answer (Ok note) ->
      Go (Sent note)
  | Sending, Answer (Rejected reason) ->
      Go (Failed reason)
  | Sending, Answer (Failed error) ->
      Go (Failed (Remote.error_to_string error))
  | _ ->
      Ignore

let update state event : (state, event) Machine.next =
  match (state.phase, event) with
  | (Editing | Failed _), Typed email ->
      Go { email; phase = Editing }
  | _ ->
      Machine.map_next
        (fun phase -> { state with phase })
        (phase_step state.email state.phase event)

let machine =
  Machine.make ~id:"signup"
    ~init:{ email = ""; phase = Editing }
    ~name:(fun state ->
      match state.phase with
      | Editing ->
          "editing"
      | Sending ->
          "sending"
      | Sent _ ->
          "sent"
      | Failed _ ->
          "failed"
    )
    ~during:(fun state ->
      match state.phase with
      | Sending ->
          [ Remote.call subscribe state.email (fun answer -> Answer answer) ]
      | Editing | Sent _ | Failed _ ->
          []
    )
    update

let view state : event Node.t =
  match state.phase with
  | Sent note ->
      <p>(Node.text note)</p>
  | Editing | Sending | Failed _ ->
      <div>
        <input
          aria_label="Email"
          value=state.email
          disabled=(state.phase = Sending)
          on_input=(fun email -> Typed email)
        />
        <button on_press=Send disabled=(not (Machine.can machine state Send))>
          "Subscribe"
        </button>
        ( match state.phase with
        | Failed reason ->
            <p>(Node.text reason)</p>
        | Editing | Sending | Sent _ ->
            Node.list []
        )
      </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

The button is disabled when the machine cannot handle [Send], for example while
the address has no [@]. So the checker sees no button that does nothing.

{1:spec The spec}

The spec of a machine is what a checker run found: the states it
reached, the resources of each, and the steps it took with the names
of their events. It is in the [specs] field of the report of {!Check.run} (see
{!Check.type-report}), and {!Silica.Spec.to_quint} and {!Silica.Spec.to_dot}
export it. See {!page-checker}.

{1:next Next}

- {!page-components}: the view, JSX and child components.
- {!page-actions}: what a machine asks the host to do.
- {!page-checker}: check every state that the machine can reach.
